## Statement

Let $T$ be a compact torus acting smoothly on a compact oriented smooth $n$-manifold $M$ without boundary, with the action preserving the orientation. Write $F=M^T=\coprod_jF_j$ for its fixed locus and $i_j:F_j\hookrightarrow M$ for each connected component. Give each normal bundle $\nu_j$ a $T$-equivariant orientation and orient $F_j$ by $o(TM|_{F_j})=o(TF_j)\wedge o(\nu_j)$. Use rational Borel equivariant cohomology, $R=H^*(BT;\mathbb Q)$, the multiplicative set $S$ generated by nonzero character classes, and the degree-shifting fiber-integration convention of the preceding definition. For every homogeneous $\alpha\in H_T^k(M;\mathbb Q)$,

$$\int_M\alpha=\sum_j\int_{F_j}\frac{i_j^*\alpha}{e_T(\nu_j)}\quad\text{in }S^{-1}H^{k-n}(BT;\mathbb Q).$$

If the fixed locus is isolated, use the ambient orientation on each normal space $\nu_p=T_pM$ and the positive orientation on each point $p$. Necessarily $n=2m$. Choose a $T$-invariant complex structure on each $T_pM$ and let $\chi_{p,1},\ldots,\chi_{p,m}$ be its nonzero character weights. Let $\epsilon_p=+1$ when its complex orientation agrees with the orientation of $M$ at $p$ and $-1$ otherwise. Then the same formula specializes to

$$\int_M\alpha=\sum_{p\in M^T}\frac{\epsilon_p\,i_p^*\alpha}{\prod_{a=1}^{m}u_{\chi_{p,a}}}\quad\text{in }S^{-1}R.$$

This is a finite-dimensional formula for compact manifolds; it does not assert a formula for arbitrary noncompact instanton spaces or infinite-dimensional field spaces.

## Statement references

[Atiyah and Bott, §3, Theorem 3.5 and equation (3.8)](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)

[Libine, §7.7, Theorem 71 and its derivation, PDF pp. 42–44](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0709.3615)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A compact torus $T$, a compact oriented smooth $T$-manifold $M$ without boundary, $\alpha\in H_T^k(M;\mathbb Q)$, the specified fixed-component orientations, the local Borel/Gysin/Euler conventions, and the exact planned Borel-localization input [L1] below as a conditional premise.

[F1] [[def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions]]: $H_T^*(X;\mathbb Q)$ is Borel cohomology, $R=H^*(BT;\mathbb Q)$ is a polynomial domain, $S$ is generated by nonzero character classes, and $\int_X$ is the oriented Borel-fiber pushforward.

[F2] [[lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities]]: for each fixed-component inclusion, $i_j^*i_{j*}^T(\beta)=e_T(\nu_j)\smile\beta$, the restriction to a disjoint fixed component is zero, and $\int_Mi_{j*}^T(\beta)=\int_{F_j}\beta$.

[F3] [[lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility]]: the fixed locus is a finite union of compact smooth components, and every $e_T(\nu_j)$ is a unit in $S^{-1}H_T^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)$, with the rank-zero case giving $e_T=1$.

[L1] [[thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions]] (planned future-only; carrier and current Step 1 readiness are absent, so this is a conditional supplier use): the proposed contract states that for a compact smooth $T$-manifold with rational coefficients, restriction $H_T^*(M;\mathbb Q)\to H_T^*(M^T;\mathbb Q)$ becomes an isomorphism after localization at the multiplicative set generated by nonzero characters, with the precise compactness/finite-type hypotheses to be completed in that item. Here $M$ is compact finite-dimensional and $M^T$ is the compact fixed submanifold from [F3]. The supplier's proof is not represented as proved or accepted in this item.

## Proof

1.1 Localize the restriction map $i^*:H_T^*(M;\mathbb Q)\to H_T^*(F;\mathbb Q)$ at $S$. By [L1] it is an isomorphism in this compact smooth finite-type case. Let $\beta=i^*\alpha=(\beta_j)_j$ under $H_T^*(F)=\bigoplus_jH_T^*(F_j)$. [given, L1]

1.2 By [F3], each $e_T(\nu_j)$ is invertible after localization. Form $\gamma=\sum_j i_{j*}^T(\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j))\in S^{-1}H_T^*(M;\mathbb Q)$; the sum is finite because $F$ has finitely many components. [given, F3, step 1.1, construct]

1.3 For a component $F_\ell$, pull back $\gamma$. The $j=\ell$ term is $e_T(\nu_\ell)\,\beta_\ell/e_T(\nu_\ell)=\beta_\ell$ by the self-intersection identity in [F2]; each $j\ne\ell$ term restricts to zero by the disjoint-support identity in [F2]. Hence $i^*\gamma=\beta=i^*\alpha$. Since the localized restriction map in step 1.1 is injective, $\gamma=\alpha$. [given, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2]

2.1 Apply $\int_M$ to the identity in step 1.3. The pushforward identity in [F2] gives

$$\int_M\alpha=\sum_j\int_Mi_{j*}^T(\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j))=\sum_j\int_{F_j}\beta_j/e_T(\nu_j),$$

which is the fixed-component formula in the localized coefficient ring. If $F=\varnothing$, [L1] says $S^{-1}H_T^*(M)=0$, so $\int_M\alpha$ localizes to zero; because $R$ is a domain and every element of $S$ is nonzero, $R\hookrightarrow S^{-1}R$ is injective and the empty sum is indeed zero. If a normal rank is zero, its Euler denominator is $1$ by [F3], so that term is the ordinary integral over that fixed component. [given, F1, F2, F3, L1, step 1.3]

3.1 If each fixed component is a point $p$, then $\nu_p=T_pM$ has no trivial weight and hence $n=2m$. With the stated ambient normal and positive point orientations, its complex character-line decomposition has Euler class $e_T(T_pM)=\epsilon_p\prod_{a=1}^m u_{\chi_{p,a}}$ by [F1]. Substituting into step 2.1 and using $\epsilon_p^{-1}=\epsilon_p$ yields the isolated-fixed-point formula. If $n=0$, the product is empty and the same calculation reads as the sum of the oriented point evaluations. [given, F1, F3, step 2.1] ∎
