## Statement

Let a compact torus $T$ act smoothly on a compact smooth manifold $M$ without boundary, and let $F=M^T$. Then $F$ is a compact smooth embedded submanifold, its connected components $F_j$ are finite in number, and $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$ for every $p\in F$. The normal bundle $\nu_j$ of each component has no trivial $T$-representation in any fiber. Give $\nu_j$ a $T$-equivariant orientation, write $c_j=\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb R}\nu_j$, and choose an invariant complex structure on it; let $\epsilon_j\in\{+1,-1\}$ compare that complex orientation with the chosen orientation. If the nonzero characters of the normal representation on a fiber, counted with multiplicity, are $\chi_{j,1},\ldots,\chi_{j,c_j/2}$, then the component of $e_T(\nu_j)$ in $H^0(F_j;\mathbb Q)\otimes R$ is

$$\epsilon_j\prod_{a=1}^{c_j/2}u_{\chi_{j,a}}.$$

Consequently $e_T(\nu_j)$ is invertible in $S^{-1}H_T^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)$. When $c_j=0$, the product is empty, equals $1$, and the Euler class is already a unit.

## Statement references

[Atiyah and Bott, §3, printed pp. 8–9, equations (3.7) and following argument](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)

[Libine, §7.7, PDF pp. 42–43](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0709.3615)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A compact torus $T$ acting smoothly on compact $M$ and the equivariant normal-bundle orientations and Thom/Euler conventions of the Statement.

[A1] $F=M^T$; each connected component $F_j$ has compact finite-dimensional cohomology, and $\nu_j$ is its $T$-equivariant normal bundle.

[F1] [[def-abbv-equivariant-integration-conventions]]: $R=H^*(BT;\mathbb Q)$, $S$ is generated by the nonzero character classes $u_\chi$, and $e_T(E)$ is the zero-section pullback of a fiber-normalized Borel Thom class.

[F2] [[lem-abbv-equivariant-thom-gysin-identities]]: every oriented normal bundle of a compact $T$-fixed submanifold has a fiber-normalized Borel Thom class; its zero-section pullback is the equivariant Euler class.

## Proof

1.1 Average a Riemannian metric over normalized Haar measure on $T$. At $p\in F$, the exponential map of this invariant metric is $T$-equivariant and is injective on a sufficiently small ball in $T_pM$. Thus, for $v$ in that ball, $\exp_p(v)$ is fixed by every $t\in T$ exactly when $v$ is fixed by every linear map $d_pt$; the fixed set near $p$ is the exponential image of $(T_pM)^T$. Therefore $F$ is locally a smooth submanifold and $T_pF=(T_pM)^T$. The fixed locus is closed in compact $M$, hence compact; its connected components are open in this locally connected manifold and compactness gives only finitely many. [given, A1, construct]

1.2 The orthogonal complement of $(T_pM)^T$ in $T_pM$ is the normal fiber, so it contains no $T$-fixed vector. The complexification of an orthogonal representation of the abelian group $T$ is a commuting family of unitary maps and decomposes into simultaneous character eigenspaces; complex conjugation pairs the $\chi$ and $\bar\chi$ spaces. A real one-dimensional character would have image in $\{+1,-1\}$ and is trivial because $T$ is connected. Thus every nontrivial real summand is two-dimensional. Averaging the representation against each character gives smooth projections onto the finitely many weight subbundles along each $F_j$. [given, step 1.1, construct]

1.3 If $\nu_j$ has nonzero weights, choose $X\in\mathfrak t$ outside the finite union of their kernels. On a real $\chi$-weight subbundle, the infinitesimal action $A_X$ satisfies $A_X^2=-d\chi(X)^2 I$, so $J_\chi=A_X/d\chi(X)$ is an invariant complex structure; their direct sum gives one on $\nu_j$. Its complex orientation may agree or disagree with the chosen normal orientation, with comparison sign $\epsilon_j$. The weight projections vary continuously, so their ranks, and therefore the character multiplicities, are constant on connected $F_j$. If there are no weights, then $\nu_j=0$ and the complex structure is vacuous. [given, A1, step 1.2, construct]

2.1 The torus acts trivially on $F_j$, hence $ET\times_TF_j\cong BT\times F_j$ and the Künneth isomorphism over $\mathbb Q$ gives $H_T^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)=H^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)\otimes R$. Restrict $e_T(\nu_j)$ to any point of $F_j$. The normal representation is the direct sum of the complex character lines in step 1.3; the Euler class of a direct sum is the product because the product of the normalized Thom classes restricts to the positive generator on each direct-sum fiber and hence is its normalized Thom class. By [F1] each line has Euler class $u_{\chi_{j,a}}$, and the orientation comparison gives $f_j=\epsilon_j\prod_a u_{\chi_{j,a}}$ as the degree-zero-in-$H^*(F_j)$ component. Every factor is nonzero and belongs to $S$, so $f_j$ is a unit in $S^{-1}R$. [given, F1, F2, step 1.3, algebra]

2.2 Write $e_T(\nu_j)=f_j+n_j$, where $n_j$ has positive degree in $H^*(F_j;\mathbb Q)$. Since $F_j$ is compact of finite dimension, every product of sufficiently many positive-degree cohomology classes vanishes; choose $N$ with $n_j^N=0$. Then in the localized ring

$$e_T(\nu_j)^{-1}=f_j^{-1}\sum_{q=0}^{N-1}(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^q,$$

as multiplication by $1+f_j^{-1}n_j$ telescopes to $1-(-f_j^{-1}n_j)^N=1$. If $c_j=0$, $n_j=0$ and $f_j=1$, giving the empty-product case as well. [given, step 2.1, algebra] ∎

