n form $QV$ is the $Q$-exact deformation, $S_t:=S+tQV$, and $E_t:=e^{-S_t}$, with the exponential defined by the usual pointwise exponential of its degree-zero part and the finite exterior-degree expansion of its positive-degree part. Require $(Q^2V)E_t=0$ for every $t\\in J$; the stronger condition $Q^2V=0$ is sufficient. Require $O E_t$ and $O V E_t$ to be admissible integrands, with no boundary flux in $I_\\Gamma(Q(O V E_t))$.\n\nWrite $(O E_t)_{[n]}=f_t\\mu$. The deformation data are analytically admissible when $f_t$ is integrable for every $t\\in J$, $t\\mapsto f_t(x)$ is differentiable for almost every $x$, $\\partial_t f_t$ is measurable, and for every compact $K\\subset J$ there is a single nonnegative $g_K\\in L^1(M,\\mu)$ such that $|f_t(x)|\\le g_K(x)$ and $|\\partial_t f_t(x)|\\le g_K(x)$ for every $t\\in K$ outside one $\\mu$-null set. The underlying manifold is finite-dimensional; its smooth-function algebra need not be finite-dimensional as a vector space.",
