+## Example
+
+In the positive-FI stable complex ADHM quotient with $N=1$ and $k=1$, take $V=W=\mathbb C$ and the datum $B_1=B_2=J=0$, $I=1$. It is stable and satisfies the complex moment-map equation. The unique fixed point is the one-box partition $Y_1=\{(0,0)\}$; its tangent character is $t_1+t_2$, its equivariant Euler class is $\epsilon_1\epsilon_2$, and its contribution to the degree-one coefficient of the fixed-point series is $(\epsilon_1\epsilon_2)^{-1}$.
+
+## Example references
+
+[[thm-adhm-fixed-point-instanton-count|Fixed-point classification and tangent character]]
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** $N=k=1$, $V=W=\mathbb C$, $\zeta>0$, the complex ADHM equation $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$, the stability condition, and independent rotation weights $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2$.
+
+[F1] [[def-framed-u-n-instanton-moduli-and-adhm-data]]: for $V=W=\mathbb C$, stability is equivalent to $I\neq0$ and the complex moment map is $IJ=0$.
+
+[F2] [[thm-adhm-fixed-point-instanton-count]]: the fixed points for $N=1,k=1$ are indexed by the one one-box partition, and their tangent character is computed from the ADHM deformation complex.
+
+## Verification
+
+1.1 The chosen datum has $IJ=0$, so the complex moment-map equation holds. Since $I=1$ maps $W$ onto $V$, its image already spans $V$, so the datum is stable. The $GL(V)=\mathbb C^\times$ action removes the nonzero scalar choice of $I$; the quotient coordinates are $B_1,B_2$. [given, F1, algebra]
+
+1.2 There is exactly one partition of $k=1$ for $N=1$, namely $Y_1=\{(0,0)\}$, so [F2] gives the unique fixed point. Here $\mathbf V_{\vec Y}=e_1$ and $\mathbf W=e_1^{-1}$, whence $\mathbf V^\vee\mathbf V=1$, $\mathbf V^\vee\mathbf W^\vee=1$, and $t_1t_2\mathbf W\mathbf V=t_1t_2$. Substitution in the tangent character gives $t_1+t_2$; the two additive weights are $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2$, so the Euler class is $\epsilon_1\epsilon_2$. [given, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 The degree-one coefficient is the sum over the single fixed point, so it equals $(\epsilon_1\epsilon_2)^{-1}$. This is a formal equivariant fixed-point contribution, not an asserted continuum path-integral value. [given, F2, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
