+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** $N\geq1$, $k\geq0$, $\zeta>0$, the Hermitian spaces $V,W$, the complex ADHM equation, the stability condition, and the $T$-action stated above. All characters are treated as algebraically independent formal variables.
+
+[F1] [[def-framed-u-n-instanton-moduli-and-adhm-data]]: the ADHM equation is $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$, stability means the $B_1,B_2$-words applied to $I(W)$ span $V$, the quotient is by $GL(V)$, and $T$ acts by the displayed rotation and dual-framing convention.
+
+[F2] [[thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula]]: the current statement is the rational Borel fixed-component and isolated-fixed-point formula for compact oriented smooth $T$-manifolds without boundary. It does not cover noncompact instanton spaces.
+
+[L1] [[thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions]]: the planned statement is that for compact smooth $T$-manifolds, rational or real Borel restriction to the fixed locus becomes an isomorphism after inverting nonzero characters. This item has no carrier in the current batch-8 manifest. The current proof of [F2] uses it conditionally, so [F2] is not treated as an accepted proof input here.
+
+[A1] For the Gieseker clause in the Statement, assume there is a $T$-equivariant isomorphism from $\mathcal M^\zeta_{k,N}$ to the specified framed torsion-free-sheaf moduli, preserving the charge grading and tangent representations. This is the unresolved local ADHM/monad correspondence obligation; it is not a proved supplier in this batch.
+
+[A2] For the pushforward clause in the Statement, assume a noncompact equivariant pushforward is separately defined on $\mathcal M^\zeta_{k,N}$ and a theorem proves its localization identity with exactly these fixed points, restrictions, and tangent Euler classes. The compact theorem [F2] alone does not supply this premise.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 The infinitesimal $GL(V)$ action and the derivative of the complex ADHM equation form the complex
+$$C^0=\operatorname{End}(V)\xrightarrow{d_0}C^1=\operatorname{End}(V)t_1\oplus\operatorname{End}(V)t_2\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(W,V)\oplus\operatorname{Hom}(V,W)t_1t_2\xrightarrow{d_1}C^2=\operatorname{End}(V)t_1t_2,$$
+where $d_0(\xi)=([\xi,B_1],[\xi,B_2],\xi I,-J\xi)$ and $d_1(\dot B_1,\dot B_2,\dot I,\dot J)=[\dot B_1,B_2]+[B_1,\dot B_2]+\dot I J+I\dot J$. Expanding gives $d_1d_0(\xi)=[\xi,[B_1,B_2]+IJ]=0$, so this is a complex. [given, F1, algebra]
+
+1.2 If $d_0(\xi)=0$, then $\xi$ commutes with both $B_i$ and $\xi I=0$. Therefore $\xi B_{i_1}\cdots B_{i_m}I(w)=B_{i_1}\cdots B_{i_m}\xi I(w)=0$ for every word and $w$. Stability says those vectors span $V$, hence $\xi=0$ and $d_0$ is injective. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+1.3 To show $d_1$ is onto, use the nondegenerate trace pairing on $\operatorname{End}(V)$. If $\Theta$ annihilates its image, variation of $\dot J$ gives $\Theta I=0$, and variations of $\dot B_1,\dot B_2$ give $[\Theta,B_1]=[\Theta,B_2]=0$. The same word calculation as in step 1.2 then gives $\Theta=0$. Thus the annihilator of $\operatorname{im}d_1$ is zero, so $d_1$ is surjective. [given, F1, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]
+
+1.4 The zero set of the complex equation is a smooth submanifold near a stable point by the implicit function theorem applied to the surjective derivative $d_1$. The $GL(V)$ stabilizer is trivial: any stabilizer element commutes with each $B_i$ and fixes $I(W)$, so it fixes every vector in the stable spanning set. Its orbit tangent is $\operatorname{im}d_0$. Choose a submanifold slice in the zero set tangent to a complement of $\operatorname{im}d_0$ in $\ker d_1$; the derivative of the action map from $GL(V)$ times this slice is an isomorphism, so the inverse function theorem gives a local quotient chart. Consequently the quotient is smooth, its tangent is $\ker d_1/\operatorname{im}d_0$, and its complex dimension is $(2k^2+2kN)-k^2-k^2=2kN$. [given, step 1.2, step 1.3, algebra]
+
+2.1 At a $T$-fixed quotient point, stability makes the gauge transformation relating any torus transform of a representative to that representative unique. Uniqueness and the group law make these transformations a representation of the complexified torus on $V$. Its weight decomposition starts with the images of the $N$ framing lines under $I$, of weights $e_\alpha$, and each $B_i$ advances the corresponding weight by $t_i$. Thus all weights in $V$ have the form $e_\alpha t_1^i t_2^j$ with $i,j\geq0$. The $J$-equivariance equation would require a weight $e_\alpha t_1^{-1}t_2^{-1}$ in this span; algebraic independence excludes such a weight, so $J=0$. The complex moment equation then gives $[B_1,B_2]=0$. [given, F1, step 1.4, construct]
+
+2.2 For each framing line, the commuting pair $B_1,B_2$ makes its cyclic span a quotient of $\mathbb C[x,y]$, with $x,y$ acting by $B_1,B_2$. The torus weights $e_\alpha t_1^i t_2^j$ are distinct between colors and between monomials, so the kernel is a monomial ideal. Its standard monomials form a finite downward-closed subset of $\mathbb Z_{\geq0}^2$, hence a Young diagram $Y_\alpha$. Stability says the cyclic spans together exhaust $V$, so $\sum_\alpha|Y_\alpha|=\dim V=k$. Conversely, for any such tuple, take one basis vector per box, let each $B_i$ move a box one unit in direction $i$ and send it to zero when the next box is absent, let $I$ send the $\alpha$th framing vector to the origin box of $Y_\alpha$ when nonempty, and set $J=0$. Downward closure makes the shifts commute, the moment equation holds, and every box is generated from its origin. These data are stable and recover the given diagrams. The construction and recovery are inverse on gauge orbits, proving the fixed-point bijection. [given, F1, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+3.1 At a fixed point the torus representations in the complex of step 1.1 are $C^0=\mathbf V_{\vec Y}^{\vee}\mathbf V_{\vec Y}$, $C^1=(t_1+t_2)\mathbf V_{\vec Y}^{\vee}\mathbf V_{\vec Y}+\mathbf V_{\vec Y}^{\vee}\mathbf W^\vee+t_1t_2\mathbf W\mathbf V_{\vec Y}$, and $C^2=t_1t_2\mathbf V_{\vec Y}^{\vee}\mathbf V_{\vec Y}$. Exactness at $C^0$ and $C^2$ follows from steps 1.2 and 1.3, so the tangent character is $[C^1]-[C^0]-[C^2]$, which is precisely the expression in the Statement. [given, F1, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+3.2 The fixed-point bijection in step 2.2 is discrete. A zero character in the tangent representation would give a nonzero $T$-fixed tangent vector and hence a positive-dimensional fixed locus through that point, contradicting discreteness. Thus every tangent weight is nonzero and its first Chern class is invertible in $K$. The tangent dimension is $2kN$, so the Euler product has $2kN$ factors counted with multiplicity. [given, step 1.4, step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra]
+
+4.1 For each fixed $k$, there are finitely many partitions of $k$, hence finitely many $N$-tuples of total size $k$; every summand $E_{\vec Y}^{-1}$ is in $K$ by step 3.2. Therefore the coefficient is a finite sum and the sequence of coefficients defines the formal power series $Z_N^{\mathrm{fp}}(q)\in K[[q]]$. At $k=0$, $V=0$, the empty tuple is the unique fixed point, the Euler product is empty and equals $1$, so the constant coefficient is $1$. [given, step 2.2, step 3.2, algebra]
+
+4.2 If assumption [A1] is supplied, the equivariant isomorphism sends fixed points bijectively to fixed points and identifies their tangent representations, so the same partitions and Euler products describe the Gieseker space. If assumption [A2] is supplied, its stated noncompact localization identity, evaluated using steps 2.2 and 3.1, gives the same fixed-point sum for that pushforward. These are conditional deductions only. The current compact ABBV statement [F2] cannot itself be applied to $\mathcal M^\zeta_{k,N}$: for $k>0$, replacing $B_1$ by $B_1+c\,\mathrm{id}_V$ preserves the equations and stability while changing $\operatorname{tr}(B_1)$ by $kc$, so it gives an unbounded family of distinct quotient classes. The missing compact/noncompact localization and ADHM correspondence inputs remain unresolved. [given, A1, A2, F2, step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra] ∎

