## Statement

Assume AC. Let \(T=(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)^r\) act smoothly on a compact finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable manifold \(M\) without boundary, let \(F=M^T\), and use the product Milnor Borel construction with coefficients \(k\in\{\mathbb Q,\mathbb R\}\). Let \(\mathscr S_T(k)\) be the multiplicative set generated by the first Chern classes of all nontrivial smooth characters of \(T\). Then
\[
\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(M\setminus F;k)=0.
\]
Equivalently, every class in the Borel equivariant cohomology of the nonfixed locus is annihilated by some finite product of nontrivial character classes.

## Statement references

[Orbit isotropy and localized support] [Atiyah–Bott, Section 3, printed pp. 5–8](https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/MAT570Spring2008/BOOKS/atiyahbott_moment.pdf)

[Finite slice-cover proof of localization] [Dwivedi et al., Section 9.10, Theorem 9.50 proof, PDF pp. 86–87](https://www.math.toronto.edu/~jeffrey/mat1312/symplecbookpub.pdf)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** AC, the compact smooth torus action, its fixed set \(F\), and \(k\in\{\mathbb Q,\mathbb R\}\).

[A1] Full AC supplies the choice hypotheses of the fixed-locus, slice, quotient, and Milnor constructions. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]

[F1] The fixed set has an invariant tubular disk neighborhood; each orbit has an equivariant tube \(T\times_H D(V)\), and every stabilizer in that tube is contained in its proper orbit stabilizer \(H\). [[lem-qftx-compact-torus-fixed-locus-normal-weights]] [[lem-qftx-compact-torus-equivariant-slice-neighborhoods]]

[F2] Every proper closed subgroup \(H\subsetneq T\) is annihilated pointwise by some nontrivial smooth character \(\chi\) of \(T\). [[lem-qftx-proper-closed-subgroup-has-annihilating-character]]

[F3] \(H_T^*(W;k)\) is the singular cohomology of \(ET\times_TW\), is a module over \(A_T(k)\), and \(\mathscr S_T(k)\) contains every nontrivial character class. The real Borel interface comes from the inspected supplier; its current readiness remains conditional. [[def-qftx-character-localization]]

[F4] For a closed subgroup \(H\subseteq T\), the restricted Milnor space gives a numerable principal \(H\)-bundle model for \(BH\). If \(\chi|_H=1\), its associated complex line is trivial and has a nowhere-zero section; its Euler class, hence its first Chern class, vanishes by the nowhere-zero-section proposition and naturality. [[thm-milnor-join-model-is-a-contractible-free-g-space]] [[thm-principal-bundles-are-classified-by-maps-to-bg]] [[thm-quotient-manifold-by-a-closed-lie-subgroup]] [[prop-a-nowhere-zero-section-forces-the-euler-class-to-vanish]] [[def-euler-class-by-zero-section-pullback-of-the-thom-class]] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]] [[thm-naturality-orientation-sign-and-whitney-product-for-euler-classes]]

[F5] Singular cohomology has Mayer–Vietoris for invariant open covers, and localization preserves exact sequences of modules. [[thm-mayer-vietoris-sequence-in-singular-cohomology]] [[lem-qftx-module-localization-preserves-exactness]]

[F6] A closed subspace of a compact space is compact. [[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]]

## Proof

**Given:** The data in the statement.

1.1 If \(F\ne\varnothing\), use [F1] to choose an equivariant tubular disk \(D_\rho(\nu_F)\) and let \(K=M\setminus D_{\rho/2}(\nu_F)\), where \(D_{\rho/2}\) denotes the image of the smaller open disk. This is closed in compact \(M\), hence compact by [F6]. Every \(x\in K\) is nonfixed, so its stabilizer \(H_x\subsetneq T\) is a proper closed subgroup. If \(F=\varnothing\), set \(K=M\); it is compact and every point is nonfixed. If \(F=M\), then \(K=\varnothing\). For each \(x\in K\), [F1] gives an open invariant orbit tube \(V_x\) with all stabilizers contained in \(H_x\); compactness gives a finite subcover \(V_1,\ldots,V_m\) of \(K\). For \(F\ne\varnothing\), the sphere bundle \(Q=S_{\rho/2}(\nu_F)\subset K\) is compact: cover the compact base by finitely many relatively compact bundle charts; over each compact chart closure the sphere fiber is compact, and the finitely many resulting compact sets cover \(Q\). [A1, F1, F6, given, construct]

2.1 Consider any invariant open set \(W\subseteq V_j\cong T\times_{H_j}D(V_j^0)\). Its inverse image in the slice has the form \(T\times_{H_j}W_0\) for an \(H_j\)-invariant open \(W_0\subseteq D(V_j^0)\). The Borel identification is the homeomorphism \([e,[t,w]]\mapsto[e\cdot t,w]\) from \(ET\times_T(T\times_{H_j}W_0)\) to \(ET\times_{H_j}W_0\), with inverse \([e,w]\mapsto[e,[1,w]]\); the quotient relations show both maps are well-defined. The projection to \(BT\) factors through \(ET/H_j\). By [F2], choose nontrivial \(\chi\) with \(\chi|_{H_j}=1\). The pullback of \(L_\chi\) to \(ET\times_{H_j}W_0\) has the global section \([e,w]\mapsto[e,w,1]\): replacing \((e,w)\) by \((eh,h^{-1}w)\) leaves this class unchanged because \(\chi(h)=1\). Thus that line bundle is trivial, and its first Chern class vanishes by [F4]. Naturality makes \(c_1(L_\chi)\) act as zero on \(H_T^*(W;k)\). This element becomes invertible in \(\mathscr S_T(k)\), so the localized module is zero. [F2, F3, F4, given, construct]

3.1 Let \(W_j\subseteq V_j\) be any finite family of invariant open subsets, with each \(V_j\) an orbit tube as above. We prove \(\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(\bigcup_{j=1}^m W_j;k)=0\) by induction on \(m\). It holds for \(m=1\) by step 2.1. For \(m>1\), put \(A=\bigcup_{j<m}W_j\) and \(B=W_m\). By induction the localized cohomology of \(A\) is zero, and step 2.1 gives zero for \(B\). The intersection \(A\cap B\subseteq V_m\) is invariant and open, so step 2.1 also gives zero for it. The Borel quotients of invariant open sets are open, since the quotient map is open (the saturation of an open set is the union of its \(T\)-translates). Thus Mayer–Vietoris applies to the Borel open cover; [F5] says its localization remains exact. The segment from the localized \(H^q(A\cap B;k)\) through \(H^{q+1}(A\cup B;k)\) to the localized \(H^{q+1}(A;k)\oplus H^{q+1}(B;k)\) has zero end terms, forcing the middle term to vanish in every degree. [F5, step 2.1, induction, algebra]

4.1 Apply step 3.1 with \(W_j=V_j\) and then with \(W_j=V_j\cap D_\rho(\nu_F)\); this shows the open union \(V=\bigcup_jV_j\) and \(V\cap D_\rho(\nu_F)\) have zero localized equivariant cohomology. The punctured tube \(D_\rho(\nu_F)\setminus F\) equivariantly deformation retracts onto \(Q\) by radial interpolation \(v\mapsto((1-t)\lVert v\rVert+t\rho/2)v/\lVert v\rVert\). The paths stay in the disk because both endpoint radii are strictly below \(\rho\). The same tubes cover \(Q\subset K\), so step 3.1 and homotopy invariance give zero localized cohomology for the punctured tube. Now \(M\setminus F=V\cup(D_\rho(\nu_F)\setminus F)\), and their intersection is covered by the open sets \(V_j\cap(D_\rho(\nu_F)\setminus F)\subseteq V_j\); step 3.1 makes this intersection localized-acyclic as well. A final localized Mayer–Vietoris sequence gives \(\mathscr S_T(k)^{-1}H_T^*(M\setminus F;k)=0\). If \(F=\varnothing\), the finite tube union covers all \(M\) and step 3.1 applies directly; if \(F=M\), the complement is empty. [F1, F5, step 3.1, given, algebra] ∎

